            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is pretty good for air leakage, however there are some nuissance areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping/door sweeps along the exterior doors, and around the attic hatch. 



The two doors in the entrance area are quite poor for air sealing, which is certainly a reason why that room is so cold. I recommend replacing the weather stripping and ensuring there is good contact when these doors are closed. Consider installing an adjustable door striker if the door has too much 'play' and does not close tightly. 



Air leakage was also observed through all the bathroom exhaust fans (this includes the utility fan in the upstairs living room). I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



For the attic, I recommend using caulking to seal up the access hatch. There was quite a bit of leakage observed around the acces hatch, this may also explain why the smoke detector located by the attic hatch has been such a nuissance. The air leakage creates a natural draft which may be pulling from the kitchen and living room area, as they are relatively close. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The main exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 640&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;18.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house appears to have 8.5 inches (R28) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 6.5 inches (R22) of blown cellulose insulation over top of the exisiting fiberglass batts. This will bring the nominal insulation to a total of R50. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6.5 inches (R22) of blown cellulose insulation over top of the exisiting fiberglass batts to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 280&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modeled adding 2.5 inches (R12.5) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation, as well as 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. 



Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 framing with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. In conjuction with the main wall, I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the pony walls. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2.5 inches (R12.5) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation and 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation to the basement walls, and 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;36.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank, which is also indirectly heated by a loop from the boiler. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

As you currently have a fairly efficient oil boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water year round. Winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year, and the remaining 3 months is typically when the boiler would be shut down. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your current oil fired boiler to heat your hot water all year, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By upgrading your boiler to a 95% condensing propane fired boiler (and continuing to heat your hot water using the boiler), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save an additional $ 240&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>&lt;b&gt;Condensing Propane Boiler Upgrade&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 84% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,750&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;41.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Savings (with current oil furnace):&lt;/b&gt; 

By using your current pellet stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,700 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



If you decide to install a second CSA certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, as well as a dedicated combustion air duct.  Which would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air Supply&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;High Velocity - Fan Coil&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



I recommend to have someone look at the High Velocity fan coil installed. During the time of the assesment, the unit appeared to be running constantly (always on), and does not appear to have a control panel connected (the control wires appear to be spliced together, which would explain why it is constantly running). Your fan coil unit also has a fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum and there is an internal damper that you can turn to control the anmount of fresh air entering your home. A good indicator for how much this should be opened is the level of humidity in the house. Too much humidity means you open it further, and too dry inside means you can close it further.





As this unit is constantly running (and bringing in fresh air), you may be over ventilating your home and bringing in more fresh air than is required, therefore increasing the heating load. If possible, I suggest a control system be installed, in which it will recognize when there is a demand for heat from the boiler, but also have the option to turn the fan on manually. As the intake is located centrally by the stairwell, this is a great option for circulating heat around the home while the pellet stove is running. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord

Pellets - $5.51 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

